cactus of doom wrote:
> Not what you WANT it to include, but what you think it probably will
> have (whenever it rolls around).

Bugs will be fixed.  That's about all we can say for sure.

Pudding farming seems like not only a game-breaking abuse, but one
that's the source of a good bit of game instability in extreme
circumstances, so I imagine that'll be fixed.

Beyond that, it's hard to say.  The past few updates have been
relatively light when compared with updates of the past, it appears to
me.  The changes made to Nethack between 3.0 and 3.1 were enough to
make it a different game in many ways: Hell was made into Genhennom,
Quests (and Quest Artifacts) were added, the Bell, Book and Candle
requirement introduced, the Marduk vs. Moloch plot was added
(downgrading poor Rodney from Amulet guardian to mere librarian), the
Sanctum was added, and the endgame switched over to the Elemental
Planes + Astral.

That was a pretty major improvement for a Nethack point release.  Since
then, one role has been added (Monk), another changed beyond
recognizability (Elf -> Ranger), races were added, one minor dungeon
branch was installed (Sokoban), weapon and magic skills added, a
handful of variants of some of the special levels and... mostly that's
it.  Sure, there's riding now, and two-weaponing, but those are still
fairly minor.  The game itself is still overwhelmingly recognizable as
that produced by the change from 3.0.

It is my hope that the next version will contain enough changes to make
us multiple-ascenders less cocky.  We all know so much about the game
now that we're just _asking_ to be taken down a peg.  Put us in our
place, Dev Team!

Beyond that, I would point out that a number of the improvements made
in Nethack+ were added to the main game. That's where we got weapon
skills, Monks and some aspects of the magic system as it stands.  So it
may be worth looking at the New Nethack+, aka Slashem, for more.
Another place to look would be the user-created patch archives (Pasi's
being a good one).

But again, there's no real way to know, except that if it's listed as a
bug on nethack.org, it'll probably be fixed.

